\n[Reviewed by\n\nPeter Rabinovitch\n\n, on\n\n05/4/2018\n\n]\n\nStochastic calculus is to random phenomena what ordinary (differential) calculus is to deterministic phenomena. It is an essential part of every probabilist’s toolkit, and can (and is) applied to many fields, most notably finance.\n\nMuch like how calculus can be taught at many levels of rigour/generality, the same can be said for stochastic calculus. This book explains stochastic calculus rigorously, but at the level of integrating with respect to Brownian motion, i.e. not in the full generality of semi-martingales. For that, see for example. Le Gall’s Brownian Motions, Martingales, and Stochastic Calculus. This book is analogous, say, to Spivak's Calculus, as opposed to something like Royden’s Real Analysis.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n500 - Sciences naturelles et mathématiques                                                                 Le Livre suisse 2019/25 (https://ead.nb.admin.ch/web/sb-pdf/2019/sb201925.pdf)\nciteturn533search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.2 years ago; Crawled: 7.2 years ago; **Baldi**, Paolo, 1948-. – Stochastic calculus : an introduction through theory and exercises / Paolo Baldi. – Cham, Switzerland : Springer, [2017]. – xiv, 627 Seiten : Illustrationen. – (Universitext) ... Baetens, Martin Kutrib (eds.). – Cham : Springer, [2018]. – VIII, 142 Seiten : Illustrationen, Diagramme. – (Lecture notes in computer science ; 10875) (LNCS Sublibrary: SL1 - Theoretical Computer Science and General Issues)\n3. Folge ; volume 68)  \nISBN 978-3-319-77660-6\n\n3708                                                                 NB 17967418003976  \n**Borg**, Ingwer. – Applied Multidimensional Scaling and Unfolding / by Ingwer Borg, Patrick J.F. Groenen, Patrick Mair. – 2nd ed. 2018. – Cham : Springer International Publishing, 2018. – 1 Band. – (SpringerBriefs in Statistics, ISSN 2191-544X)  \nISBN 978-3-319-73470-5\n\n3715     